 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p>All the Navy sources are public study aids published by squadrons of the Chief of Naval Air Training (CNATRA) for one training aircraft each, cited by PDF page. They are not flight manuals, and each says so or defers to NATOPS.</p>
 <p id="source-vt6"><strong>[1] Training Squadron SIX (VT-6), How to Handle an Emergency Situation.</strong> Undated, T-6B. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw5/vt6/assets/docs/university/how-to-handle-eps.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 1–2 and 4.</p>
 <p id="source-vb"><strong>[2] Training Squadron TEN (VT-10), Introduction to Briefing Guide, 18 June 2026.</strong> T-6A. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/vt10/assets/docs/training/ep-brief-guide.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 3–5.</p>
 <p id="source-vs"><strong>[3] Training Squadron TEN (VT-10), FAM Stan Notes, June 2026.</strong> T-6A. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/vt10/assets/docs/training/stan-notes-fam.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, p. 5: simulated emergencies, turn, climb, clean and the dead-engine glide rule.</p>
 <p id="source-e6"><strong>[4] Training Air Wing SIX, US16LA-JPATS Low Speed Ejection Seat: seat brief and aeromedical aspects of ejection.</strong> Undated, T-6. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/assets/docs/safety/aero-and-egress-T6.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 2–3.</p>
 <p id="source-e45"><strong>[5] Training Air Wing SIX, NACES SJU-17: aeromedical aspects of ejection and emergency ground egress.</strong> Undated, T-45. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/assets/docs/safety/aero-and-egress-T45.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 2–3.</p>
 <p id="source-pe"><strong>[6] Training Air Wing SIX, Post-Ejection Procedures (T-6).</strong> Undated. <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw6/assets/docs/safety/post-ejection-procedures-T6.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 1–2.</p>
 <p id="source-t44"><strong>[7] Training Squadron THIRTY-FIVE (VT-35), T-44 Emergency Phrases, December 2024.</strong> <a href="https://www.cnatra.navy.mil/tw4/vt35/assets/docs/training/t44-emergency-phrases.pdf">cnatra.navy.mil</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 1–2.</p>
 <p id="source-afman"><strong>[8] US Air Force, AFMAN 11-202 Volume 3, Flight Operations, 10 January 2022, incorporating Change 1 of 25 June 2026.</strong> <a href="https://static.e-publishing.af.mil/production/1/af_a3/publication/afman11-202v3/afman11-202v3.pdf">Published on the Air Force e-Publishing site</a>, which states no releasability restrictions; read 9 November 2026 from a copy supplied for this revision, by paragraph: 3.20.2 and Attachment 1 (minimum and emergency fuel).</p>
 <p id="source-cnaf"><strong>[9] US Navy, CNAF M-3710.7, NATOPS General Flight and Operating Instructions Manual, 5 May 2016.</strong> Read 9 November 2026 from a copy supplied for this revision, by paragraph: 7.1.7 (ditching and bailout) and 8.2.1.1 (aircrew equipment, the life preserver note). Summarised in this site's own words, as the manual restricts reproduction for nonmilitary use.</p>
 <h3>Supplementary: FAA</h3>
 <p id="source-afh"><strong>[10] Federal Aviation Administration, FAA-H-8083-3C, Airplane Flying Handbook, chapter 18, Emergency Procedures.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/regulations_policies/handbooks_manuals/aviation/airplane_handbook/19_afh_ch18.pdf">faa.gov chapter PDF</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 18-1–18-21. Written for civil light aircraft; a US Government work.</p>
 <p id="source-aim"><strong>[11] Federal Aviation Administration, Aeronautical Information Manual, chapter 6, section 3, Distress and Urgency Procedures.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap6_section_3.html">faa.gov</a>, read 9 November 2026, by paragraph: 6-3-3, ditching procedures.</p>
 <h3>Sim source</h3>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[12] Falcon BMS, BMS Training Manual 4.38.1.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-Training-Manual.pdf">cdn.falcon-bms.com</a>, read 9 November 2026 by printed page: p. 14 (the three rules), p. 59 (ejection), pp. 143–146 (mission 7, flameout landing). A sim manual: cited for the In the sim panel, and for the key positions and the F-16 heights in Figure 1, labelled as such.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/emergency-procedures-evidence.md">Emergency Procedures evidence</a>. No Army source was read: Army emergency procedures were not found in a public, readable form. The worked example's flight is invented; its numbers are the T-6's from [1] to [3].</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: military pilots of every service, fixed-wing; helicopters through the Autorotation module. Each type's flight manual and checklist govern its emergency procedures, limits and ejection decision. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
